yoyo test and yoyo run <cmd> now exit with the status of what they ran, so a script or CI step can trust them. It's committed as 6d84deff, and the full build, test, clippy and fmt checks all passed.

What I checked by hand, using the debug binary in a temp directory outside the repo:

| Command | Exit code |
|---|---|
| yoyo test, project with a failing test | 101 (cargo's own code) |
| yoyo test, same project once the test passes | 0 |
| yoyo run false | 1 |
| yoyo run true | 0 |
| yoyo test with no project | 1 |
| yoyo run with no command (just prints usage) | 0, unchanged |

What changed
• **src/commands_run.rs:** new `ShellOutcome` and `shell_exit_code`. Passed gives 0. A failure keeps the child's own exit code, or 1 if it was killed by a signal or has no usable code. Nothing ran gives 1. handle_run is now a thin wrapper over handle_run_with_status.
• **src/commands_lint.rs:** `handle_test` is now a thin wrapper over `run_tests`, which takes the outcome from the child's real exit status rather than parsing the summary text. The REPL /test and /run keep their signatures and output.
• **`src/dispatch_sub.rs:** the test and run arms call a small exit_if_failed` helper. It flushes stdout and exits only on a nonzero code, so a passing run takes exactly the old path. The file grew to 2164 lines, inside its 2166-line size allowance.
• **`docs/src/usage/commands.md`:** one paragraph stating the exit-code rules.

Tests: a table test runs the real mapping function, with the pass row (still 0) checked exactly. A second test runs real commands (true, false, exit 3, kill -9 $$) and expects 0, 1, 3 and 1. For the control check, I made the mapping always return 0 and both tests failed by name; after I restored it they passed, and the NEUTERED marker is gone. I wrote no test that runs yoyo test inside this repo, since that would run the repo's own test suite again.

Left open on purpose: yoyo run with no command still exits 0. An existing in-process test calls that path, and exiting there would kill the whole test run. It's listed in #982, along with the other shell subcommands that print a failure but still exit 0:
• `dispatch_sub.rs still has 40 return Some(None)` sites across 37 subcommands, all of which exit 0.
• `lint, diff, blame and tree <missing>` were confirmed in the assessment; the rest aren't checked yet.
• #982 also flags find/grep with no match as a decision to make, not a bug. However, my scan didn't list find or grep as subcommands at all, so it's unconfirmed that those two exist as shell commands.
